I see two distinct ways in which NetHack's RNG makes save-and-restore
substantially different to the savefile capability in eg, Quake:

1) (more or less what you point out above)

Combat in Quake: You didn't move fast enough to frag the scrag before it
killed you.  Try performing exactly the same moves again, and practice
makes perfect - you'll eventually get the timing right.

Combat in NetHack: You weren't lucky enough (out of game luck) to whack
the mumak to death before it killed you.  You have as long as you want
to think about each move, so performing exactly the same moves again
means that the only difference is the result of the dice roll - and no
matter how improbable it is for that method to succeed, as long as it's
not absolutely *impossible*, it will eventually work.

2)

Maps in Quake:   Every game has the same map - if you die and start
again, you'll have to trudge through the same stuff you've done before
(probably many times) in order to get to whatever it was that killed
you.  So you might as well restore from a savefile.

Maps in NetHack: Every game has different maps (apart from a few special
levels) with monsters appearing at different times and locations.  If
you die and don't restore, you now get to play a completely different
game, that you don't know like the back of your hand, to reach (another
one of) whatever it was that defeated you before - which can be
frustrating, but hardly boring.

